in @prism

# @epistemologic/property/frame_relativity
#
# A type is frame-relative iff its values' meaning depends on an
# observer's frame, and cross-frame comparison requires explicit
# conversion. Timestamps are the canonical instance: a wall-clock
# duration measured at one shard does not directly compare to a
# wall-clock duration measured at another shard; both must be
# converted to a shared frame first.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-time-as-substrate-and-postgres-heritage.md
# §6 (the property table): this encodes PG §2.1 (timestamptz over
# timestamp). PostgreSQL learned that storing local time without a
# frame is almost always wrong; the substrate inherits the lesson
# at the algebra layer. The load-bearing instance is @time.wall and
# @time.instant; @mirror/shard.self provides the frame.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-shard-as-observer-relative-lambda-zero.md:
# the shard IS the observer-relative λ₀; its parent: option(oid)
# chain is the frame-conversion path. Comparing two wall durations
# from different shards is a cross-observer operation that resolves
# through the nearest common ancestor shard. This is
# relativity-of-simultaneity at the substrate.
#
# Sibling properties: monotonicity(T) (within-frame total order —
# the property that's free once you ARE in a single frame),
# causality(T) (the partial order that cross-frame conversion must
# preserve).

grammar @epistemologic/property/frame_relativity {

  # The type carries a frame: every value is annotated with the
  # observer whose frame it lives in. For @time.wall/@time.instant
  # this is the shard. Verifier checks the type's declaration
  # exposes a frame field or is reachable through zoom(T) from a
  # frame-bearing carrier.
  carries_frame(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # Cross-frame compare requires explicit conversion. The verifier
  # walks every comparison callsite (==, <, >) of the type's values;
  # any callsite where the two operands have differing frames
  # without an intervening convert in scope fails the property.
  # PG's lesson §2.1: never compare local-frame values silently.
  cross_frame_compare_requires_convert(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # The conversion path between any two frames is well-defined.
  # For the shard-rooted frame system: any two shards reach a
  # common ancestor through the parent: option(oid) chain, OR they
  # don't (disjoint roots), in which case convert returns imperfect.
  # The substrate guarantees the lookup terminates because the
  # chain is finite (per @epistemologic/property/autopoietic: the
  # ancestor chain is a content-addressed DAG with no cycles).
  conversion_path_defined(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # The combined property: the type is frame-relative.
  # Pass iff carries_frame, cross_frame_compare_requires_convert,
  # and conversion_path_defined.
  frame_relativity(type) -> verdict { \ }
}

out carries_frame
out cross_frame_compare_requires_convert
out conversion_path_defined
out frame_relativity
out @epistemologic/property/frame_relativity
